From dae6b01daa535fb2ed98aa8ae06092c90321b5fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Peripheral Pretender setup <pp-setup@localhost>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 13:29:49 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] hw/arm: PIC32CM standby: GCLK channel gating, CPU clock, WFI
 hook

Standby as PIC32CM MC00 silicon showed it (firmware/hello
APP=sleepprobe and sleepprobe2, checked by sleep): a WFI with
PM.SLEEPCFG = STANDBY stops every GCLK peripheral channel that no
enabled peripheral with RUNSTDBY asks for, and the CPU clock; sources
and generators run on demand whatever their own RUNSTDBY, and every
peripheral on a running channel counts, RUNSTDBY or not. What still
runs (RTC, WDT, EIC, peripherals with RUNSTDBY) wakes the core.

- target/arm: ARMCPU.sleep_hook, called with the BQL when WFI halts
  the CPU and when arm_cpu_exec_halt() leaves that halt.
- The SoC hooks it: it reads SLEEPCFG, finds the channels to keep from
  each instance's RUNSTDBY and ENABLE (new MchpInstanceInfo standby_*
  fields, generated from the ATDF's CTRLA/CTRL), and calls
  mchp_gclk_set_standby() and mchp_mclk_set_standby(). Channels with no
  such instance keep running. A "standby" QOM property does the same
  for tests; a reset leaves standby.
- armv7m_systick: a stopped CPU clock holds the count (ptimer_stop)
  instead of setting a zero period, which disabled the timer for good;
  cpuclk changes are ignored while SysTick runs on refclk, as the
  comment there intended.
- TC and TCC fold the ticks since their last event into the count when
  their clock changes rate, and a clock coming back from 0 restarts
  the time base at that moment (the TC used to count the whole standby
  on waking up).

qtest pic32cm-pm-test: channels, a shared channel, SysTick.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
